  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA strong surface high is building in today out of the northwest\nleading to good radiational cooling tonight. Dew points have \nfallen into the mid to low 40s which will likely match the low \ntemperatures tonight. Temperatures for the overnight hours were \nlowered below model guidance to match close to what the current \ndew points are reading. It is possible to get a few areas of \npatchy frost, mainly in Northern Oneida and in a few elevated \nvalleys of Delaware and Otsego counties. Weak warm air advection\nbegins tomorrow as a strong upper level ridge builds in with a \ngradual warm up expected through Wednesday bringing some late \nsummer temperatures back to the region. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \n\nModels have the upper level ridge flattening out Thursday into \nthe weekend with quick zonal flow setting up. Deterministic \nmodels have many weak 500 mb shortwaves passing through over the\nlate week into the weekend so rain showers and thunderstorm \nchances increase. Given that there is no major shortwave and not\na whole lot of moisture for these weaker waves to work with, \noverall rainfall amounts will likely not be a wash out or an all\nday rain but precipitation chances have been increased Thursday\nas well as this weekend. Thursday may have a small chance at \nsome stronger storms as well as shear will be present and some \nof the modeled forecast soundings do have some surface \ninstability. Right now instability is fairly low but will need \nto be monitored as we get closer in time.",
